# Drone Missions — environment variables
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values. .env.local is
# gitignored and must never contain committed secrets.
#
# The full set below is validated at boot by the Zod schema in
# src/lib/env.ts (fail-fast: a missing required var or a malformed value
# throws immediately with a readable error instead of failing later, deep
# inside a request handler). See that file for the exact rules/defaults.

NODE_ENV=development

# --- Database / Flyway ------------------------------------------------------
# Optional in this phase: no Supabase project is wired up yet, so the app,
# build, and test suite all run fine with these unset. Once a Supabase
# project exists, fill both (see MIGRATION_PLAN.md §8):
#
#   DATABASE_URL — postgres.js connection string, Supavisor SESSION mode pool
#     DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:[password]@[project-ref].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
#   FLYWAY_URL — JDBC connection string Flyway CLI uses to run db/migration/
#     FLYWAY_URL=jdbc:postgresql://[project-ref].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# Local alternative — the docker-compose.yml `db` service (a plain
# `postgres:16` container) instead of a hosted Supabase project. These are
# exactly that service's committed credentials (docker-compose.yml's `db`
# service: user/password `postgres`, database `dronemissions`, published on
# localhost:5432), so pasting them as-is works once `docker compose up db` is
# running:
#     DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/dronemissions
#     FLYWAY_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/dronemissions
#
# Left commented out, not set to an empty value: Next's dotenv loader treats
# `DATABASE_URL=` as the *string* "", not "unset", and src/lib/env.ts's Zod
# schema rejects an explicitly-empty optional string — so uncomment and fill
# these in only once you actually have a value, rather than leaving the
# `KEY=` line in place.
# DATABASE_URL=
# FLYWAY_URL=

# --- JWT ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required. Must be at least 32 bytes (HS256 requirement) — e.g.
# `openssl rand -base64 32`. No default is shipped on purpose: unlike the
# Spring backend's dev-fallback secret, a working default here would mean
# every clone of this repo signs tokens with the same publicly-known key.
JWT_SECRET=

# Token lifetime in milliseconds. Defaults to 86400000 (24h) if unset.
JWT_EXPIRATION_MS=86400000

# --- Mail (Resend) -------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults to false: EmailService logs the rendered email instead of sending
# it, so the app works with no credentials. Flip to true to send for real.
MAIL_ENABLED=false
MAIL_FROM=DroneMissions <no-reply@dronemissions.app>
# Required only when MAIL_ENABLED=true. Left commented out rather than
# `RESEND_API_KEY=` for the same empty-string-vs-unset reason as
# DATABASE_URL/FLYWAY_URL above — env.ts rejects an explicitly empty value.
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# --- Mission cache (ports app.cache.mission.*) -----------------------------------
# The read-through cache in front of the mission data-access layer. Defaults
# below are the Spring backend's: 5 minutes TTL, 1000 missions, 200 cached
# query results (smaller, because the key space includes a free-text keyword
# filter). MISSION_CACHE_ENABLED=false removes the decorator entirely — the
# query module is then used directly, with no caching code on the path.
MISSION_CACHE_ENABLED=true
MISSION_CACHE_TTL_MS=300000
MISSION_CACHE_MAX_SIZE=1000
MISSION_CACHE_LIST_MAX_SIZE=200

# Dev testing: when set, EVERY email is delivered to this one inbox instead
# of the real recipient, and the subject is prefixed with the address it was
# meant for (`[→ someone@example.com] New bid on "..."`). Useful with
# MAIL_ENABLED=true against a Resend sandbox key without mailing real users.
# Unlike the vars above, an empty value is meaningful and allowed here: blank
# (the default) means normal delivery to the real recipient, so this line can
# safely stay as-is. Mirrors Spring's `app.mail.redirect-to`.
MAIL_REDIRECT_TO=

# --- App base URL --------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute origin this app is reachable at. Used to build the CTA links inside
# emails (`${APP_URL}/missions/{id}`). Defaults to http://localhost:8085 —
# localhost on the PORT below, since this app serves its own email links.
# Keep the two in step: if you change PORT, change this to match, or every
# email CTA points at a port nothing is listening on.
# Replaces the Spring backend's `app.frontend-url` / `FRONTEND_URL`: that
# pointed at the separate Angular dev server on :4200, whereas here the Next
# app serves the UI and the API from one origin. Set it to the deployed
# origin (e.g. https://dronemissions.example.com) in any non-local environment
# — emails sent from production with the default would link to localhost.
APP_URL=http://localhost:8085

# --- Scheduled jobs -------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret for the HTTP trigger of the nightly overdue sweep
# (GET /api/cron/overdue-sweep). Only needed on a SERVERLESS host.
#
# On a container (Dockerfile / docker-compose) nothing here applies: the sweep
# is owned by the in-process node-cron timer that src/instrumentation.ts starts
# at boot, and this variable can stay unset.
#
# On Vercel that timer can never fire (no long-lived process), so vercel.json's
# `crons` entry calls the route instead. Set CRON_SECRET in the Vercel dashboard
# and Vercel automatically sends `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` with every
# cron request. Any long random value works: `openssl rand -base64 32`.
#
# The route FAILS CLOSED — unset or empty, it answers 401 and sweeps nothing,
# because an unauthenticated GET here would notify and email every overdue
# pilot. Read straight from process.env rather than through src/lib/env.ts (a
# host-owned variable, like NEXT_RUNTIME, and keeping it out of the eagerly
# parsed schema keeps a bad value from 500ing every /api/v1/* request through
# the Edge middleware). Left commented out rather than `CRON_SECRET=` for the
# same empty-string-vs-unset reason as DATABASE_URL above.
# CRON_SECRET=

# --- Server --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults to 8085, mirroring the Spring backend's server.port.
PORT=8085

# --- Logging (pino) -------------------------------------------------------------
# One of: fatal, error, warn, info, debug, trace, silent.
# Defaults to "info" in production and "debug" otherwise when unset. Left
# commented out rather than `LOG_LEVEL=` for the same reason as
# DATABASE_URL/FLYWAY_URL above — env.ts's z.enum() rejects an explicitly
# empty string too.
# LOG_LEVEL=
